Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Three Bowls Above the Canal Gauge
- Story & Context: The debut opens at 116 BPM with Élodie sustaining three glass tones separated by a fifth and a major second. Pascal establishes a bright six-note guitar cycle while Mireille answers in the spaces between his accents. The glass harmony does not change for the first thirty-two bars. Instead, the two guitar patterns rotate through increasingly complex inversions while Chantal repeats the same two-note likembe figure throughout. Jonas enters only after the fourth cycle with one damped bass note at each phrase ending. At the midpoint, Élodie adds a fourth bowl, creating a slow beating interval that changes the emotional color of the entire groove without a conventional chord change. The final section pushes both guitars into double-time sebene figures while the glass tones remain almost motionless.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Night hydrology lab overlooking a dark canal, three illuminated glass bowls reflected in an analog water-level gauge, two guitar necks crossing in opposite directions above cream chart paper. No full lyrics; sparse spoken fragments limited to “three bowls,” “same level,” “add one line,” and “do not drain it.”

Track 2: The Likembe Refuses the New Pattern
- Story & Context: Pascal and Mireille begin with a paired guitar figure at 122 BPM, then deliberately change their interlock every eight bars. Chantal is instructed not to change her likembe part at all. Her fixed metallic cell becomes the listener’s reference point while the guitars move through syncopated triads, high single-note runs, and brief unison peaks. Élodie uses only two glass pitches, alternating which one receives stronger finger pressure so the harmonic field seems to breathe without changing notes. Jonas contributes dry bass droplets on irregular phrase endings. Near the climax, the drum kit drops to rim and shaker while the guitars become most active, proving that the rhythmic engine lives in the string weave rather than percussion density.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Close view of a small likembe resting on a water-test bench while two overlapping guitar-pattern diagrams are repeatedly crossed out around it, glass armonica bowls blurred in the background. Spoken fragments limited to “keep your pattern,” “we move around you,” “same metal,” and “hold the pin.”

Track 3: Capillary Rise Stops at Drawer Forty-Two
- Story & Context: The closer begins with a single near-silent glass tone that slowly blooms as Élodie increases fingertip pressure. One guitar enters with harmonics, the second with muted chord fragments. Each eight-bar section adds rhythmic activity but only one new harmonic pitch. The music therefore rises vertically rather than progressing through standard chords. Jonas gradually removes his bridge foam during the piece, allowing bass notes to lengthen slightly as the glass field becomes brighter. In the final minute, the guitars reduce to one repeated offbeat dyad while Élodie removes glass tones in reverse order. The last remaining bowl fades naturally as its rotation slows. Chantal closes the record with one dry likembe pluck recorded without reverb.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Old hydrology filing drawer labeled 42 pulled open beneath a glass capillary tube with water visibly risen inside it, a spinning glass bowl reflected in stainless steel, muted guitar strings at frame edge. Spoken fragments limited to “rise to forty-two,” “one level at a time,” “slow the wheel,” and “leave one drop.”

